WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.779
Hollywood has a long, long history of putting
its coolest characters from one movie smack

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:08.590
dab in the middle of another movie's plot,
and these days, every little film property

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.090
wants to be a big, tough expanded universe.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:15.910
But what about the more obscure crossovers,
the ones that took years for fans to discover?

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:19.010
Here are the coolest crossovers in film that
you may have missed.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.140
Caddyshack, Ghostbusters, &amp; Casper

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.060
When Harold Ramis co-wrote the original Ghostbusters
movie, he managed to sneak one pretty amazing

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.600
crossover cameo from one of his earlier movies:
1980's Caddyshack.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.559
We all remember crazy greenskeeper Carl Spackler
from Caddyshack, played to perfection by Bill

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:36.559
Murray.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:40.289
In a deleted scene from Ghostbusters, the
now-homeless Spackler can be seen walking

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:44.519
through Central Park with another bum, played
by Dan Aykroyd, debating politics and martial

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.080
arts.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.920
"I think that a good karate guy is ALWAYS
going to top a heavyweight boxer."

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.620
Eleven years later, director Brad Silberling
got in on the crossover action, adding a brief

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.049
scene into Casper which features Dan Aykroyd's
Ghostbusters character running away in terror

00:00:58.049 --> 00:01:00.199
from the haunted Whipstaff Manor.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:03.940
"Who you gonna call?

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:04.940
Someone else."

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.360
Alien, Blade Runner, Firefly and beyond

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.980
Did you know that some of the most beloved
science fiction franchises in existence are

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:13.980
all related?

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.190
While it's long been speculated that there's
a connection between the Alien and Blade Runner

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.780
worlds, the idea was essentially confirmed
with the DVD release of Prometheus.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.680
In one of the bonus features on the disc,
you can read a letter from Peter Weyland in

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.080
which he talks about his mentor in robotics,
and how it eventually all "blew up in his

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.080
face."

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.500
For those who know their Blade Runner history,
it's pretty clear that Weyland is referring

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.230
to Eldon Tyrell, who got killed by one of
the replicants he created.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.820
Those movies also have a link to the 1998
sci-fi film Soldier, which is considered an

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.530
unofficial sequel to the original Blade Runner.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.540
For example, in one scene you can see a Blade
Runner spinner in a trash heap.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.570
Additionally, die-hard fans of Joss Whedon's
series Firefly can tell you that the logo

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.470
on Mal's turret gun in the pilot episode belongs
to none other than the Weyland-Yutani corporation

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:00.840
from the Alien franchise.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.360
Finally, there's the whole Predator connection,
which was explored in two completely unnecessary

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.780
Alien vs. Predator movies, but hinted at much
earlier in Predator 2, when a xenomorph skull

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.269
is seen in a trophy case.

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:14.269
Every Pixar movie

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:17.389
Pixar has produced a whole lot of great movies.

00:02:17.389 --> 00:02:21.310
But did you know that all of those films are
at least interconnected, if not set within

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.689
the same universe?

00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:27.749
According to movie critic Jon Negroni's ridiculously
in-depth theory, it goes deeper than that.

00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:32.959
He says the world of Pixar movies is run by
mega-corporation Buy n Large, or BnL, which

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:37.620
shows up in multiple movies, and all the movies
exist on the same timeline.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.879
Beyond Negroni's theory, the connections can
be traced through an impressive number of

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:44.889
Easter eggs the Pixar animators have left
sprinkled through their films, including the

00:02:44.889 --> 00:02:49.700
ever-popular "Pizza Planet" truck, which appears
in almost every Pixar movie.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.480
Rango &amp; Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.840
While we're on the topic of animated movies,
let's talk about Rango.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.680
This 2011 children's feature film from Gore
Verbinski has much more in common with Fear

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.419
and Loathing in Las Vegas than you might realize.

00:03:03.419 --> 00:03:07.060
Both movies feature Johnny Depp in the main
role, and Rango wears a flowered shirt much

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.590
like Hunter S. Thompson's alter-ego "Raoul
Duke" from Fear and Loathing.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.600
At one point, Rango gets plastered to the
windshield of a passing vehicle, driven by

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:19.060
Thompson and his lawyer "Dr. Gonzo" in the
same convertible from Fear and Loathing.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.730
Additionally, there's a scene in Fear and
Loathing where Thompson sees a bunch of clothed,

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:23.849
humanoid lizards.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:28.139
Given the themes of existentialism throughout
Rango, it makes you wonder if maybe the entire

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:32.150
movie may have taken place in the hallucinations
of Thompson as he drove through the desert.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.549
"Suddenly there was a terrible roar all around
us.

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:39.940
And the sky was full of what looked like huge
bats, all swooping and screeching and diving

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:40.940
around the car."

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.400
Split &amp; Unbreakable

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.260
Director M. Night Shyamalan's career seems
to be taking an upswing.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.160
His 2015 found footage horror movie The Visit
was a huge success, and Shyamalan followed

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:55.390
that up with another hit, the 2016 psychological
thriller Split.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.609
For fans of Shyamalan's earlier work, the
way that Split ended was explosive.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:02.180
"This is like that crazy guy in the wheelchair
that they put away 15 years ago."

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.750
"They gave him a funny name, too."

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.370
"Mr. Glass."

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:13.170
Yup, that's a reference to Samuel L. Jackson's
wheelchair-bound villain from Unbreakable.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.389
This appearance by Willis was more than just
a quick bit of fanservice.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:20.730
The cameo was cut from test screenings in
order to keep it a secret, and Shyamalan revealed

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:25.510
that the character of Kevin actually appeared
in early versions of the script for Unbreakable.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:29.840
That makes Split a sequel to Unbreakable,
set some fifteen years later.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.140
If that weren't exciting enough on it's own,
Shyamalan is hard at work on a third movie,

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.810
Glass, which will feature the characters from
both Split and Unbreakable.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:38.810
Thanks for watching!

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.130
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.350
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

